Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> 
> 
> Instead of messing with a skew to make
> ML = floor((XL + DLVL + SKEW) * (1/2))
> how about messing with the multiplier, so that
> 
> trivial sets ML = max(floor((XL + DLVL) * (1/3)),1)
> easy    sets ML = max(floor((XL + DLVL) * (2/5)),1)
> medium  sets ML = floor((XL + DLVL) * (1/2))
> hard    sets ML = floor((XL + DLVL) * (3/5))
> insane  sets ML = floor((XL + DLVL) * (3/4))
> 
> This has the advantages for the harder cases of not
> putting an impossible starting step at DLVL 1,
> while letting the ramp up effect run to even higher
> hastening of the arrival of the hardest monsters.
> 
That should work better, though I'd go for event
multiplier increments.

Trivial 3/10
Easy 2/5 (4/10)
Medium 1/2
Hard 3/5
Insane 7/10

That makes Insane and Trivial symmetrical.

Of course, once you've got the amulet DLVL is replaced with the sanctum
depth. If that is, say, 50, it means that in the endgame for insane
difficulty ML is increased by 10, and reduced by 10 for the trivial
version.

> 
> Another useful modification would be to change the
> monster level limit from absolute to probabilistic,
> say by applying an absolute-of-a-gaussian distribution

A slightly more complex, but appropriate, method would
be to select out-of-depth monsters as if they'd actually
come up from lower levels.

E.g, at medium, 5% of monsters are generated  using a DLVL 1d6 greater
than actual (bounded  above by the branch depth) At insane, 7% of 
monsters have come upstairs from 1d10 levels deeper. At trivial, 3% of
monsters have come upstairs from 1d2 levels deeper.

There'd also be monsters that have come down from higher levels;
5% at medium, 7% down 1d10 levels at trivial, 3% down 1d2 levels
at insane.

